{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{3} & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot (-1)=-2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot (2)+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is *not* orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1=-5\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1=2\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)=-3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-1)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot 8=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39630" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=-2 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $H.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1)=1\\ne 0 &  & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $H.$ Notice that, again,  we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $V,H$- and $W$ as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},{{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 3=5\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $V.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\n- For the set $H:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 4=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u$ is not orthogonal to the set $W.$ Notice that we stopped after finding the first non-orthogonal vector in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -8 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{-5+(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80535" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{b}{a+c}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-10,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a+c}=\\frac{-3}{-10+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80538" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{c}{a+b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{a+b}=\\frac{2}{1+1}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-71102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+b}{c}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+b}{c}=\\frac{1+(-2)}{1}=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80537" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]$ is a non-zero  vector that is orthogonal to the set $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]},$ find the value of $\\frac{a+c}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-10{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=5,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a+c}{b}=\\frac{5+3}{4}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80532" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)=-6\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+(-6)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39631" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let  $h=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ A vector  $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to $h.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot h$ for $i=1,2,3.$   \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot h & =4\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot (-4)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-4)=30\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4)=-19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{1}$  only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe