& =1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 3=10\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot h & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3=-4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot h & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80576" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{3}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$ \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$  \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$ \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 4=19\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =7\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 4=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =7\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-3)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-80584" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{3}$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]}.$ Which of the following vectors belong to ${H}^{⊥}?$\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nNow, a vector $v$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $v$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products ${v}_{i}\\cdot {h}_{j}$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For ${v}_{1}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=-64\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{1}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{2}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+(-14)\\cdot 0+(-6)\\cdot 5=-60\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{2}\\notin {H}^{⊥}.$ We do not need to test ${v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{2}.$\n\n- For ${v}_{3}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-6)+14\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 5=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+14\\cdot 4+6\\cdot (-11)=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${v}_{3}\\in {H}^{⊥}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${v}_{3}$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{1}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "${v}_{3}$ only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-140468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${v}_{3}$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a basis of ${H}^{⊥}$ if  $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the  second equation we get ${x}_{2}=0$ and then from the first equation we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.$ So, any vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}"] ["math" "B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39626" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {x}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0$  and from the first one, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ So, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={0}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  ${H}^{⊥}$, if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0$  and from the first one, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ So, the solution to the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={0}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={R}^{2}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39633" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ad7d5e222d886f1e5fa9f7558591b20b499399cf14d64f974ecdfbdab2b07ff8"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{H}^{⊥}={0}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"] ["math" "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{1}\\in R}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "q-39915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/state.json" "file_sha256" "d9f02fa8029cfac6e562e946382ccd4eaa4dc2363b41416ede5d697717fc33da"} {"question" "e-5560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/b85705767814eae0f99345888b1f0c65a89dbbc76458d7093a02a20937d2e1c3.png) which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/85cd8d0ebcd70de93ec2bd5007907a02d81daadd27b348e7c72ccef53654d881.png)\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/e03fd2577b8d7067f4507d0ee6fe732d3786a494134cd967d3c65de01e28e709.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5560.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff97016cf7aaf9c904ff0ffc065975a9b275757685ef5b2f4fb1618ae89ac2"} {"question" "e-5560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot 1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)+6\\cdot (-1)=-6\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H.$\n\n- For the set $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})=0\\,✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2.$ Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V,$ we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5560.json" "file_sha256" "f3ff97016cf7aaf9c904ff0ffc065975a9b275757685ef5b2f4fb1618ae89ac2"} {"question" "e-5561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d648a1f36d090c680a6b72f30ff447ce78623f746157348f30a02fa4ea245c66.png) is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d29f02d59f540cae1aae6057e05eeeb349d84de1ac9be11f6e3b131083f6905a.png) find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5561.json" "file_sha256" "50cc7f35db98381827cc1bac49b9d002d0c531ede293542f0f096f7b97cb57f2"} {"question" "e-5561" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/608fd40128068aa5f05e6b256081bd2e0ebbf569fca4c9bae4977740eb4db2ce.png)\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3.$ So, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/d6d7ea3c500d1112c64e19da288fbd7ad7e6ed912cf3121a2292427368b19f0e.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/c4875bc998184d934c9b43113cd31091b792f1c04ff4387d4b8ab8a7a08f90d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5561.json" "file_sha256" "50cc7f35db98381827cc1bac49b9d002d0c531ede293542f0f096f7b97cb57f2"} {"question" "e-5913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector subspace ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/bee23fc9beaddffe7fd60b2c2bbd2d2ee952a277960c5d08fcad5b934e209f64.png) Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}?$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/abfbcbc8c3b942b93faf4b48336daa54345b97fb21d3aa11685f0d29f206590e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5913.json" "file_sha256" "69f30e301b06603a0fe4486442103d999f1996768ef58f02ff68332ca099ece0"} {"question" "e-5913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$   \n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H.$ So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and  $j=1,2.$  \n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3=4\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}.$ We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2.$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{1} & =5\\cdot (-2)+8\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {h}_{2} & =5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2,$ which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5913.json" "file_sha256" "69f30e301b06603a0fe4486442103d999f1996768ef58f02ff68332ca099ece0"} {"question" "e-5562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/889411800149371acab53c44829a050d69df697ce6723dd95b9045ddf873e484.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5562.json" "file_sha256" "7b1fc4b33f7fc2cffde1ec7b1830d86011f50ccd4ea8c5412553d84a14555d26"} {"question" "e-5562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$ \n\nA vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/ed0e0a3a78ff25be588f62627ffd5bae4074f6c59b8e88ad160e4407253ae6dd.png) belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0$ and $\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/af507041d4aa3cc7dfb415afc672b5a7875f9f1a27acced81505797e5f92d7e0.png) \n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get $x_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$ Substituting this into first equation, we obtain $x_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/34b29c1902a7c441b2ac15be91ffb60b8856ab7dc2706146d09eb02f081827ba.png)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/assets/496154a4d67c0b7861da038306f1d5cc65188cc036cf8514b24603c7ce4cd948.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/example-5562.json" "file_sha256" "7b1fc4b33f7fc2cffde1ec7b1830d86011f50ccd4ea8c5412553d84a14555d26"} {"question" "q-39623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "84cdf890763c10d16b555ba5c075a4839b9e1e13ab912a207e1372e048b3fc59" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: dot products with H are 0 and 0; with V they are 0, 0, and -2; with W they are 0 and 2. Orthogonality to a set requires every dot product to vanish, so only statement I holds. Original option 0, the authentic worked solution, and Correct grading agree. The stored 'I and II only' is genuinely incorrect and must remain distinct." "bindings" {"problem" "919ef3a67efbb47e7a02b5c3a7cfd5476359d0a92c90acbe63abbed49c3b4f46" "worked_solution" "e2f079dbfef807b40a7f0dfc1b1d12efae8f0f2b4f7d546e999835421db5dd82" "original_field" "08c9445ed24f30551b036625798fc4718f84041c9aa17797d3d1a82cef8a8c06" "reviewed_field" "76669d66c1568cc05e72210c9f7388a152e1c222ed229090a4e16f551f072ed6" "raw_widget" "44eee2504777376e130de90e78c3403c21e81ac9bc8bb270717f01021c9c3962"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-39623-before.png" "sha256" "9cfd1e10b7ba8c200e01aa417f4c6b3a97cd183984d82863ae397425a55b8ca4"} {"path" "history-q-39623.png" "sha256" "5e74063adb43a8c6f809ff29c7234c700a7dfc94e17600a5eeacbecde515e133"} {"path" "q-39623-after.json" "sha256" "880cdf96936a2b04772d0cbb8e7794ed9b77b2c3c9f413f9eeac148d42ed0545"}]} {"question" "q-39623" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065672/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "84cdf890763c10d16b555ba5c075a4839b9e1e13ab912a207e1372e048b3fc59" "rationale" "Derived judgment: dot products with H are 0 and 0; with V they are 0, 0, and -2; with W they are 0 and 2. Orthogonality to a set requires every dot product to vanish, so only statement I holds. Original option 0, the authentic worked solution, and Correct grading agree. The stored 'I and II only' is genuinely incorrect and must remain distinct."} {"question" "q-39915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{H}^{⊥}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,:\\,{x}_{2}\\in R}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math