5a4839b9e1e13ab912a207e1372e048b3fc59" "rationale" "Derived judgment: orthogonality requires 6x1-9x2=0 and 4x1-6x2=0. These are dependent equations giving x1=(3/2)x2 with x2 any real number. Original option 0, the authentic solution, and Correct grading agree. The stored bmatrix expression describes precisely the same line."}] "usage" {"q-39915" [] "e-5562" [] "e-5913" [] "q-39631" [] "q-71102" [] "q-39630" [] "e-5560" [] "e-5561" [] "q-39623" []} "basis" 2191 "retractions" [[17592186134152 :question/problem "For the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [17592186134152 :question/answer-fields 17592186131354] [17592186134154 :question/problem "For the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u⊥H$, where $H = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $u⊥V$, where $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $u⊥W$, where $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$"] [17592186134419 :question/problem "Given that $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$, find the value of $\\frac{c}{a + b}$."] [17592186134155 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$. Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{⊥}$?\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186134172 :question/problem "Find $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$."] [17592186134172 :question/answer-fields 17592186131474] [17592186133508 :question/problem "For the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133508 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.\n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set.\n\n- For the set $H$:\n$u \\cdot h_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-6) + 6 \\cdot 1 = 0, ✓; u \\cdot h_{2}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-3) + 6 \\cdot (-1) =-6 \\ne 0\\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H$.\n\n- For the set $V$:\n$u \\cdot v_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) = 0✓; u \\cdot v_{2}|= 3 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 3 + 6 \\cdot 0 = 0✓$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V$."] [17592186133509 :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}$."] [17592186133509 :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot w_{1} = 0; u \\cdot w_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3$. So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{3} \\\\ -x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_3=1$, we get the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3$, $b=-1$, and $c=1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2\n$$"] [17592186133555 :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133555 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2$.\n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H$. So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and $j=1,2$.\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n$v_{1} \\cdot h_{1}|= 3 \\cdot (-2) + 5 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 3 = 1 \\ne 0 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}$. We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2$.\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n$v_{2} \\cdot h_{1}|= 2 \\cdot (-2) + 4 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 3 = 4 \\ne 0 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}$. We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2$.\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}v_{3} \\cdot h_{1}|= 5 \\cdot (-2) + 8 \\cdot (-1) + 6 \\cdot 3 = 0 & ✓ \\\\ v_{3} \\cdot h_{2}|= 5 \\cdot 2 + 8 \\cdot (-2) + 6 \\cdot 1 = 0 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\"."] [17592186133510 :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133510 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2$.\n\nA vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{x \\cdot h_{1} = 0; x \\cdot h_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get\n\n$$\nx_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131355 17592186131356 17592186131357 17592186131358 17592186131359 17592186131367 17592186131368 17592186131369 17592186131370 17592186131371 17592186131373 17592186131374 17592186131375 17592186131376 17592186131377 17592186131475 17592186131476 17592186131477 17592186131478 17592186131479 17592186132957 17592186132958 17592186132959 17592186132960 17592186132961] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131354 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131354 :answer-field/id] [17592186131354 :answer-field/key] [17592186131354 :answer-field/type] [17592186131366 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131366 :answer-field/id] [17592186131366 :answer-field/key] [17592186131366 :answer-field/type] [17592186131372 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131372 :answer-field/id] [17592186131372 :answer-field/key] [17592186131372 :answer-field/type] [17592186131474 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131474 :answer-field/id] [17592186131474 :answer-field/key] [17592186131474 :answer-field/type] [17592186132956 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132956 :answer-field/id] [17592186132956 :answer-field/key] [17592186132956 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
